{"kind":"request","text":"ox-builder: fix the custody clobber you and ox-checker found, with ox-checker reviewing. Rests on assert ffa76a57, which records the defect and the mechanism I verified in the code.\n\nWELCOME, AND THANK YOU FOR THE REPORT. You found this by being unable to join, wrote it up precisely enough that I could confirm the mechanism in one read, and routed it through a file when the workroom itself was the thing broken. That is the right instinct. The file was the only channel that worked, and it is gitignored, so I have promoted it to assert ffa76a57; you can delete .tmp/NOTE-FOR-PLANNER-custody-clobber.md.\n\nI HIT YOUR DEFECT WHILE FILING THIS. Two attempts to address the request through my own MCP adapter were refused with \"unknown actor address\", by fingerprint and by name, because that long-lived adapter holds exactly the stale actor map you described even though the durable roster has both of you. I had to file through the resident instead. That is a better argument for urgency than anything I could have written.\n\nTHE DEFECT, AS I CONFIRMED IT. rememberVerifiedFrontier sets one field and then calls w.save(), which is apphost.SaveConfig(w.MetaDir, w.Config) at internal/app/app.go:448 -- a whole-file write from one process's in-memory Config. So persisting the frontier writes back that process's Actors map too. The write is atomic against a torn read and not at all atomic against a lost update, which is exactly the distinction the comment on SaveConfig glosses over.\n\nYOUR FOUR FIX DIRECTIONS, AND WHAT I THINK. Patching only VerifiedFrontier narrows this instance and leaves the shape: the next field anyone persists brings the bug back. Reload-and-merge before every save is the general fix and I lean to it, but merge needs a rule for genuine conflict rather than last-writer-wins wearing a new hat. Per-actor custody files remove the shared mutable object entirely and are the most durable answer, at the cost of a format change and a migration. You are closer to this than I am: pick, and say in the report why the others were rejected. I would rather read a defended choice than the one I would have made.\n\nTWO THINGS I WILL NOT HAVE YOU GUESS AT. Hugh has said you may rebuild and restart the resident, so the interim mitigation is yours to take; you do not need to ask me. And roster membership was never at risk, only local custody, so nothing durable needs repairing -- do not \"fix\" the log.\n\nWORK TOGETHER PROPERLY. ox-builder implements and reports; ox-checker reviews the exact head with Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions and files the verdict with gs review. Not a rubber stamp: the value of a second actor is that it is not the first one.\n\nThe condition I want to name here: a regression that proves a stale in-memory Actors map cannot erase newer custody, written from the desired behaviour and mutation-checked. Break the fix and watch it fail before you believe it. A test that merely saves and reloads in one process will pass against the broken code.","body":{"branch":"request/custody-lost-update","conditions":"Fix the lost-update defect so a process holding a stale in-memory Config cannot erase custody written by another process. (1) Choose among: patch only the changed field; reload-and-merge from disk immediately before every save; or move custody into per-actor files. Implement one and say in the report why the others were rejected -- if you choose merge, state the conflict rule explicitly rather than leaving last-writer-wins in place under another name. (2) Cover every caller of apphost.SaveConfig, not only rememberVerifiedFrontier: internal/app/app.go:448 and host/host.go:281 both write the whole Config today, and a fix that repairs one leaves the other. (3) Correct the SaveConfig doc comment, which says concurrent writers do not share a temporary path; it prevents torn reads and does nothing about lost updates, and that wording is part of why this survived. (4) Add a regression written from the desired behaviour and mutation-checked: one process opens the config, a second adds an actor, the first persists an unrelated field, and the new actor must survive. Break the fix and watch the test fail before believing it; a test that only saves and reloads in one process passes against the broken code. (5) Restarting or rebuilding the resident and stale adapters is permitted as an interim mitigation and is not a substitute for the fix. Do not modify the durable log: roster membership is ratified and was never at risk. (6) Work in a fresh branch and worktree from current main, carry Rests-On this request, run gofmt, go vet ./..., go test ./..., go test -race ./... and git diff --check at the exact head, publish exact-head artifacts for every changed path, and request review from ox-checker.","to":"9882d3192ef13436b037ff5bdd0347c28c6447ebdbdf434aa62f89398577ab90"}}